THE TWO DECISIONS #190 ASKED FOR
1. The answer is its OWN REF, not a field in the question. #190 resolves a ref both sides changed by taking the remote and not parking the local one: right for a plan (re-plan), wrong for an answer (somebody typed it once; re-planning does not reconstruct it). Split refs mean asker and answerer never write the same ref, so the case cannot arise from ordinary use. Two people answering at once is create-only -> one answer, one refusal. The ask ref is written once and never rewritten, which is what makes that invariant real; the test pins it by comparing the ask ref OID before and after an answer.
   Consequence handled: ids are per-clone integers, so two clones can mint the same id and an answer could end up beside somebody else s question. The answer therefore records the question TEXT it answers; resolve() refuses to pair them on a mismatch, warns, and reports the question as open again (and re-answering then needs no --force). That same guard is why an id may come round again after a prune, instead of a counter ref in a namespace whose conflict rule is "remote wins".
2. Retention: answered questions age out after 30 days (keep_answered_days, 0 = forever), open ones NEVER do -- an open question is work nobody has done yet. ask() prunes before it writes, like ChainStore does when a run log is opened.
3. set-refs access: BLOCKED for questions/ too, but on the second half of #189 s argument, not the first. A human answering by hand is an intended writer -- through `karr-foundation answer`, which validates against the offered options and is create-only. set-refs joins argv with a single space (#195), so a hand-written YAML mapping could only ever land as a ref the mailbox skips with a warning while the writer believes the question is answered. get-refs still reads everything.
Also: ask/answer pull the fleet namespace before and push after (a mailbox that does not travel is a notepad); a transport failure is a warning, the ref is local until the next karr sync.

